From c8a73e8f0815d684e9bc047089af61c58b330ca1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergei Rudenkov <sergeirudenkov@google.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 09:08:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Do not attribute worker loads without a parent frame to
 the main frame

PageResourceLoader.#resolveFrameTarget walks from a non-frame initiator
target up to the owning frame target and, if the worker's TargetInfo
carries no parentFrameId, falls back to the page's main frame id.
Service workers and shared workers are parented by the page target but
are not owned by any single frame, so their TargetInfo never has a
parentFrameId. As a result, their source map loads were dispatched on
the page session as Network.loadNetworkResource(frameId=<main frame>),
which makes the browser issue the request with the main frame's origin,
site-for-cookies and credentials. A service worker registered by a
cross-origin iframe could thereby turn opening DevTools into an
authenticated, first-party GET against the embedding site.

Fail closed instead: when the initiator is a non-frame target and
neither the initiator nor any target on the way up reports a frame id,
keep the load on the initiator's own target with no frameId. The
browser then attributes the request to the worker's own origin, which
is how these loads were attributed before 510dd0d17f. Page scripts
without a frameId keep the main frame default, and dedicated workers
keep using parentFrameId.

This supersedes the walkedUpFromWorker carve-out from c0fe7533df, which
treated the main frame attribution of such worker loads as the regular
worker path and exempted it from the same-origin check, together with
the test that pinned that behavior.

